WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.780
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.600
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.460
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.740
by giving you the facts.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.840
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.740
Today we learn about an herb
with impressive health benefits

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.320
that has long been used
in alternative medicine.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.120
It’s called fenugreek and
its effects are remarkable.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.860
"The effects of a commercially
available botanical supplement

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.880
on strength, body composition,
power output, and hormonal profiles

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.740
in resistance-trained males"

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.780
Something had a significant
impact on both upper and lower body

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.600
strength and body composition
in comparison to placebo

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.460
in a double-blind
controlled trial.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.880
These changes were obtained
with no clinical side effects.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.640
It allowed these men
to leg press an extra

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.740
hundred pounds compared
to placebo.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.440
And the magical substance was...

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.600
Fenugreek.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.000
A naturally occurring
edible spice

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.720
that appears to double
as an anti-cancer agent.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.960
In summary, fenugreek
seeds may possess

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.091
potent anti-cancer
properties.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.646
In our next story, we learn
that for all the benefits

00:01:16.646 --> 00:01:21.326
of fenugreek seeds, they do
have an interesting side effect.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.640
Cancer prevention is all well and good,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.880
but there is a side effect of
fenugreek seed consumption—

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.020
it makes your armpits smell
like maple syrup.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.940
“Analysis of human male armpit sweat
after fenugreek ingestion.”

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.040
And yes, it happens
to women, too.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.760
“In this study, the strong
‘maple-syrup’ odour which appears

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.280
after fenugreek ingestion
was investigated.”

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.520
They had men plaster some
gauze pads under&nbsp;their armpits

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.400
before and after eating a tablespoon
of fenugreek seeds.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.880
Sweat was collected
over a 24-hour period,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.100
and given to a panel of eight
odor assessors to get their take.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.480
They described what they smelled as
cocoa, roast beef,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.740
carrots, spicy, gravy,
but then, honey,

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.080
rose and lilac,
peach-like, raspberry.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.640
I guess it could be worse.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.840
There was one assessor who described one of
the fenugreek odor compounds as

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.580
“[m]usty, sweet,
onion,&nbsp;socks.”

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.800
Another agreed, but thought it
more like “[f]ruity, floral, socks.”

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:35.460
Of course, there was the boring assessor who
was like, uh, smells like fenugreek.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.340
This is a
harmless phenomenon;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.820
the only reason I bring it up is that there
is actually a serious congenital disorder

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.600
called maple syrup urine disease:
branched-chain ketoaciduria.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.380
It’s completely unrelated,
but breastfeeding infants,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.600
with moms using fenugreek to boost their milk
production, may be misdiagnosed.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.660
So, if you’re pregnant or breastfeeding,
and eating fenugreek,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.165
make sure to tell your OB,
just so they don’t worry.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.523
Finally today, we look at the
hormonal benefits of fenugreek.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.280
In my video, the "Benefits of
Fenugreek Seeds," I profiled this study

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.900
in which young men were randomized
to a sugar pill or fenugreek capsules

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.040
for eight weeks and got a significant
improvement in upper body strength,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.680
lower body strength,
and body composition,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.560
a significant reduction
in body fat percentage.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.480
And the only side effect?

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.680
It can make your sweat and
pee smell like maple syrup.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.360
Seems like a bonus!

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.760
Studies on immature castrated rats
suggest the fenugreek muscle

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.680
bulking is a testosterone effect,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.140
but we didn't know what
happens in humans...until now.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:50.160
Four randomized controlled
trials put it to the test and

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.940
saw a significant boost
of total blood testosterone.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.480
And indeed, fenugreek appears
to improve sexual function in men,

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.660
for example, doubling the
frequency of morning erections.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.720
What about the sexual function
in women? While the estrogen

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.380
hormone, estradiol, stimulates
vaginal lubrication and blood flow,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.760
facilitating a woman's capacity
for sexual arousal and orgasm,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.840
it's the testosterone that's
linked with sexual desire

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.640
in both men and women.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.540
Drug companies have tried
testosterone patches on women

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:28.080
to try to increase sexual desire,
but we're concerned

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.180
about blood clots
and long-term safety.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.400
What about a little fenugreek?

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.700
A significant increase in
testosterone compared to placebo

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.380
and a boost in the estrogen estradiol,
which resulted in both an increase

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.060
in sexual desire and function,
translating into about a doubling

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.580
of sexual activity
compared to placebo.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.500
If fenugreek causes an increase
in estrogen levels, what about

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.620
the efficacy of fenugreek for
reducing menopausal symptoms?

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.800
A significant reduction in menopausal
symptoms across every single domain,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.860
for example, cutting the weekly
numbers of hot flashes and

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.460
night sweats in half over
a period of three months.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.260
Other hormonal effects include
an improvement in painful periods.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:17.460
What I like about this study is that
they didn't use some propriety extract,

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.060
but just straight, plain fenugreek
powder that you'd buy at the store.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.720
Now, they packed it into capsules
just so they could pit it against

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.240
a sugar pill placebo,
but the dose they used

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.640
is just about a third of
a teaspoon three times a day,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.620
taken the first three
days of their period.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.460
A third of a teaspoon would
cost less than 5 cents.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.340
And boom!
A significant decrease in pain,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.580
and also appeared to improve
other symptoms as well.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.680
While we're on a roll with hormonal
effects, what about the effectiveness

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.960
of fenugreek as a galactagogue?
No, not another sci-fi reboot.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.920
A galactagogue is something that
increases breast milk production

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.040
in lactating mothers, and
fenugreek fits the bill.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.900
And not just by a little.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.380
In this randomized double-blind
placebo-controlled trial in which

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.100
breastfeeding women took less
than a quarter teaspoon of fenugreek,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.800
less than a quarter teaspoon
of ground ginger,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.400
and about a 16th of a teaspoon
of turmeric every day,

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.220
doubled their milk production
within a month,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:22.380
about a 50% increase by week two
and a 100% increase by week four.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.180
However, due to its potential
stimulation on the uterus,

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.600
fenugreek seeds should not
be used during pregnancy.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.900
Though this is all based
on laboratory animal data,

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.265
better safe than sorry.

